[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r11
diagnosis_text:
No boundary link is down. The issue is a routing redistribution gap on r11, not a physical failure. Evidence:
- r11’s OSPF adjacency to the backbone is healthy on eth1 (B, 111.1.1.2): “show ip ospf neighbor” is Full with r1 (111.1.1.1), and r11 has a full OSPF table via eth1. The interface is UP in Linux, and OSPF routes are installed.
- EIGRP on r11 is also healthy: r11 has EIGRP-learned routes to 102.1.x (from r12) and 113.1.x (from r13), proving EIGRP adjacencies over eth0 (EE, 112.1.1.1) and eth2 (FF, 103.1.1.1) are up.
- From pc12, ping to r11 (112.1.1.1) works, but ping to r1 (12.1.1.1) fails. Forward path exists (r12 default to r11, r11 has OSPF routes). The return path from OSPF to 120.1.1.0/8 is missing: r1’s routing table contains no 120.0.0.0/8, because r11’s OSPF config only has “redistribute connected” and does not redistribute EIGRP 100.

Mechanism: asymmetric routing. Packets reach the backbone but replies are dropped due to lack of OSPF knowledge of EIGRP LANs (e.g., 120.0.0.0/8). Localization: r11, OSPF process on eth1 is fine; the redistribution policy in FRR is the fault. Root cause: the “redistribute eigrp 100” statement was removed or not restored during maintenance on r11. Corrective fix: on r11, under router ospf, add “redistribute eigrp 100” (optionally with a route-map/metrics), and verify r1 learns 120.0.0.0/8. No interfaces are physically down. === END ===